1
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:53,571
The dead sleep with their eyes
open, my grandfather would say.

2
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:56,366
They're watching you from the past...

3
00:01:56,449 --> 00:01:57,993
Jackie Sharp.

4
00:01:58,076 --> 00:01:59,620
Romero wasted no time.

5
00:01:59,703 --> 00:02:00,871
What good are you?

6
00:02:00,954 --> 00:02:02,998
You Democrats are full of surprises.

7
00:02:03,081 --> 00:02:04,917
Her lawyers won't let
us get anywhere near her.

8
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,044
Because they're
negotiating her testimony.

9
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:08,587
How the hell did we get here?

10
00:02:08,671 --> 00:02:11,965
Romero was privy to information.
Someone from within the party.

11
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,592
He's threatening to go
after her for whipping votes

12
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:17,888
in exchange for campaign funds
from a Mr. Remy Danton,

13
00:02:17,971 --> 00:02:20,015
who, shockingly, she was sleeping with.

14
00:02:20,098 --> 00:02:22,100
Well, she's not testifying
because of an affair.

15
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:24,144
She's testifying 'cause she
doesn't want to go to jail.

16
00:02:24,227 --> 00:02:26,354
Which is the only reason
anyone testifies.

17
00:02:27,773 --> 00:02:29,650
Doug, keep at the rest of the
Democrats on that committee.

18
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:31,359
Make sure they stay in line.

19
00:02:31,443 --> 00:02:34,404
I'll keep working my side of
the aisle, but... uh, Romero.

20
00:02:34,487 --> 00:02:35,613
He's a prick.

21
00:02:35,698 --> 00:02:38,241
Mmm-hmm, prick
who still wants whip.

22
00:02:38,325 --> 00:02:40,035
Terry Womack has been very loyal.

23
00:02:40,118 --> 00:02:42,579
Yeah, well, there's loyal,
and then there's good.

24
00:02:44,164 --> 00:02:45,916
What has Womack brought you?

25
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:48,335
Did he kill the Declaration
of War Committee?

26
00:02:48,836 --> 00:02:50,337
Did he deliver Missouri
for you in November?

27
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:51,629
That's enough.

28
00:02:52,715 --> 00:02:53,799
Let's give it to him.

29
00:02:53,882 --> 00:02:55,718
Look, sometimes you have
to make hard choices.

30
00:02:55,801 --> 00:02:57,427
I'll talk to Terry.

31
00:02:57,510 --> 00:03:00,513
Romero's gonna have to find a
place for him to land, and...

32
00:03:00,597 --> 00:03:02,515
where that is matters to me.

33
00:03:02,599 --> 00:03:03,934
That'll be all for now.

34
00:03:05,352 --> 00:03:06,353
Yes, sir.

35
00:03:16,654 --> 00:03:17,823
Put Doyle on this.

36
00:03:19,616 --> 00:03:20,951
We already vetted Romero.

37
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:23,078
No, I mean on Usher.

38
00:03:25,247 --> 00:03:27,040
<i>The body is
remarkably well-preserved,</i>

39
00:03:27,124 --> 00:03:29,752
<i>except for the extremities,
which frostbite took care of.</i>

40
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:31,211
<i>But dental is intact.</i>

41
00:03:31,294 --> 00:03:34,006
<i>Our counterparts in Antarctica
are cooperating fully,</i>

42
00:03:34,089 --> 00:03:36,549
<i>and the body will be in our
possession within the next 12 hours.</i>

43
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:38,343
<i>I'll have it in transit after customs.</i>

44
00:03:38,426 --> 00:03:41,346
We'd like the body to
get lost in transit.

45
00:03:41,429 --> 00:03:42,680
<i>Ma'am?</i>

46
00:03:42,765 --> 00:03:44,266
It needs to get lost.

47
00:03:45,183 --> 00:03:46,184
<i>Yes.</i>

48
00:03:47,685 --> 00:03:50,147
Okay. Thank you, Assistant
Director Green.

49
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:51,648
Uh, keep in touch.

50
00:03:57,654 --> 00:04:00,866
- I thought you'd gone to bed.
- Can't yet.

51
00:04:04,327 --> 00:04:06,538
- Who's that?
- No one we know.

52
00:04:10,125 --> 00:04:11,835
Tom, I said it's no one we know.

53
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:20,968
You ever hear about the
5,000-year-old man they found

54
00:04:20,993 --> 00:04:22,744
frozen in the Italian Alps?

55
00:04:23,889 --> 00:04:25,098
No.

56
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:29,311
His skull was caved in... from the back.

57
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:32,022
He probably never saw it coming.

58
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:37,694
Beaten and left for dead.

59
00:04:41,489 --> 00:04:43,867
Left for dead is an ancient concept.

60
00:04:45,577 --> 00:04:47,370
You know, when you freeze to death...

61
00:04:48,288 --> 00:04:51,624
the last thing you feel is extreme heat.

62
00:04:51,708 --> 00:04:53,168
That hurts.

63
00:04:54,169 --> 00:04:55,212
Sorry.

64
00:04:59,216 --> 00:05:00,675
I saw Francis out there.

65
00:05:02,052 --> 00:05:03,595
He seems on edge.

66
00:05:05,722 --> 00:05:06,849
Can you blame him?

67
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:10,560
Better?

68
00:05:32,875 --> 00:05:34,626
Shoplifting is the worst of it.

69
00:05:35,543 --> 00:05:39,339
A couple of recent street buys
that led to court-mandated rehab.

70
00:05:39,422 --> 00:05:44,344
That's where she lives, where she works,
where she scores...

71
00:05:44,427 --> 00:05:47,890
But all I see here is a woman in
her 20s, trying to get to 30.

72
00:05:48,974 --> 00:05:51,643
- She's a concern.
- I don't get it.

73
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:54,646
She was in touch with Gavin Orsay
before he left for Venezuela.

74
00:05:54,729 --> 00:05:56,231
- Doug...
- Nathan!

75
00:05:57,149 --> 00:05:59,734
There's a bite to your tone
that we might need to discuss.

76
00:06:02,529 --> 00:06:04,656
She's a loose end and an addict.

77
00:06:06,992 --> 00:06:08,618
That's a bad combination.

78
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:14,249
It's a clean slate.

79
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:17,460
Donald Blythe's taste was
a bit too dark for me,

80
00:06:17,544 --> 00:06:19,004
so I stripped it all back.

81
00:06:20,297 --> 00:06:23,591
I have chosen one painting so
far that's on its way, though.

82
00:06:24,467 --> 00:06:27,512
Thad Peterson's body is taken care of.

83
00:06:27,595 --> 00:06:29,431
His name is John Doe.

84
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:32,434
I'd recognize him anywhere.

85
00:06:34,602 --> 00:06:37,064
This would've been very
embarrassing to some interests

86
00:06:37,147 --> 00:06:38,565
that are important to this country.

87
00:06:38,648 --> 00:06:41,193
And I wouldn't want the
family to see him this way.

88
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:44,862
What's the news from
the Jordanian Embassy?

89
00:06:46,364 --> 00:06:51,286
Aidan Macallan still won't reveal
what he did or didn't tell Petrov.

90
00:06:51,369 --> 00:06:53,788
Well, the president wants him
brought back from his asylum

91
00:06:53,871 --> 00:06:55,248
to the United States immediately.

92
00:06:55,332 --> 00:06:56,833
I think we should give
it a little more time.

93
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:01,546
You know, I wonder if it was such a
good idea to bring you in on this.

94
00:07:01,629 --> 00:07:04,716
- You promised...
- I know. I know. Ahmadi...

95
00:07:06,551 --> 00:07:09,054
We've got Special Forces
on the ground, tracking.

96
00:07:09,137 --> 00:07:12,890
Russia's presence in Syria is making
this more difficult than I once thought.

97
00:07:12,975 --> 00:07:16,353
I need to know what
Macallan told Petrov.

98
00:07:16,937 --> 00:07:21,441
It never ceases to astonish me how
talented men are at making a mess.

99
00:07:36,289 --> 00:07:38,500
Uh, it's not gonna serve you
to go after your own party.

100
00:07:38,583 --> 00:07:40,293
You never stop.

101
00:07:41,669 --> 00:07:44,172
Jackie Sharp will never testify.

102
00:07:44,256 --> 00:07:45,798
Well, then she'll go to jail.

103
00:07:46,716 --> 00:07:49,761
You can't prove it, Alex. You
can't prove the quid pro quo.

104
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:51,013
She seems to think I can.

105
00:07:51,929 --> 00:07:54,599
If you play it a little differently, you
have a chance to get what you want.

106
00:07:54,682 --> 00:07:57,227
We're offering you whip.

107
00:07:57,310 --> 00:07:59,396
- I don't want it anymore.
- What?

108
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:01,689
Jesus Christ.

109
00:08:04,234 --> 00:08:05,693
Ways and Means, then.

110
00:08:05,777 --> 00:08:07,945
The keynote at the next convention.

111
00:08:08,030 --> 00:08:09,990
What I want is for Francis
Underwood to go to jail.

112
00:08:10,073 --> 00:08:11,241
How is that gonna help you?

113
00:08:12,575 --> 00:08:14,536
Really? How's it gonna help you?

114
00:08:19,249 --> 00:08:20,750
There's another way to go at it.

115
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:24,754
Wanna make a name for yourself?

116
00:08:24,837 --> 00:08:26,464
You wanna make the president's
life a living hell?

117
00:08:26,548 --> 00:08:28,091
Draw it out.

118
00:08:28,175 --> 00:08:30,677
Keep the committee going
but at a slow burn.

119
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:32,595
Aren't you working for him?

120
00:08:32,679 --> 00:08:35,390
Members of the administration,
you bring 'em in one at a time.

121
00:08:35,473 --> 00:08:38,810
You could ride this for a couple
of years and secure your place.

122
00:08:38,893 --> 00:08:40,770
While you keep the president in check.

123
00:08:41,771 --> 00:08:44,399
Don't end it before it's begun
by calling Jackie Sharp.

124
00:08:44,482 --> 00:08:45,858
You know I see good things for you.

125
00:08:45,942 --> 00:08:48,361
Let me plan out your next eight years.

126
00:08:48,445 --> 00:08:51,239
Hmm. The candidates you worked with...

127
00:08:53,616 --> 00:08:58,288
how many of them have waited and
waited and lost their moment? Hmm?

128
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:01,916
Yeah, that's right.

129
00:09:01,999 --> 00:09:04,502
I'm reading the moment,
and this is mine.

130
00:09:05,420 --> 00:09:07,505
Let me out on Third Street.

131
00:09:07,589 --> 00:09:10,425
- I'll drop you at Rayburn.
- No, I always walk the final 50 yards.

132
00:09:10,508 --> 00:09:12,635
Never know when you might get
a chance to press some flesh.

133
00:09:12,719 --> 00:09:13,720
- Uh-huh.
- Yeah.

134
00:09:13,803 --> 00:09:16,098
Hi, I'm Alex Romero.

135
00:09:16,806 --> 00:09:18,975
I represent the Seventh
District of Arizona.

136
00:09:19,059 --> 00:09:20,855
I want to know what
keeps you up at night

137
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:22,461
so I can work all day to address it.

138
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:24,397
The future belongs to all of us.

139
00:09:24,981 --> 00:09:26,566
I hope I can count on your support.

140
00:09:28,901 --> 00:09:30,903
What do you think, Mark? Huh?

141
00:09:30,987 --> 00:09:32,780
I mean, you're the expert.

142
00:09:34,532 --> 00:09:35,617
Right here.

143
00:09:37,244 --> 00:09:39,496
<i>Romero said no, and the
usual offers don't interest him.</i>

144
00:09:39,579 --> 00:09:41,873
Oh, he's interested. He
just hates himself for it.

145
00:09:41,956 --> 00:09:44,501
I am making headway with the
Republicans on the committee.

146
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:47,754
They all have pasts, most
of them have witnesses.

147
00:09:47,837 --> 00:09:50,132
<i>One way or another, Jackie
Sharp won't be testifying.</i>

148
00:09:50,215 --> 00:09:53,385
So why are you having so much trouble
with this one young congressman?

149
00:09:53,468 --> 00:09:54,636
He's tenacious.

150
00:09:56,138 --> 00:09:57,805
He reminds me of you.

151
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:00,558
Well, I don't see that at all.
We'll talk later.

152
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:03,603
I could reach out to Danton...

153
00:10:03,686 --> 00:10:05,730
We expose him, he'll expose us.

154
00:10:05,813 --> 00:10:09,484
- Well, then what do you suggest?
- That I go on being presidential.

155
00:10:09,567 --> 00:10:11,403
That's the only way that
I can deal with this.

156
00:10:11,486 --> 00:10:13,946
It's my first 100 days, for God's sake.

157
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:16,073
Francis...

158
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:19,244
we're going to get our hands on Ahmadi.

159
00:10:19,327 --> 00:10:21,704
There aren't enough
Ahmadis in the world.

160
00:10:21,788 --> 00:10:23,415
Not for all we've done.

161
00:10:23,498 --> 00:10:24,582
Then what?

162
00:10:25,667 --> 00:10:29,796
Destroy Romero, for one. Can
we at least take care of that?

163
00:10:29,879 --> 00:10:31,756
And then who? The rest
of the Democratic Party?

164
00:10:31,839 --> 00:10:34,217
I know what I'm doing, Claire.
I've been president before.

165
00:10:34,301 --> 00:10:35,510
So have I.

166
00:10:36,553 --> 00:10:38,263
Get me Terry Womack.

167
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:42,142
Your job as vice president is to advise.

168
00:10:42,225 --> 00:10:44,852
My job is to make the decisions.

169
00:10:46,229 --> 00:10:49,899
Terry, yes, I'm just checking in.

170
00:10:52,068 --> 00:10:55,905
Mmm-hmm. Well, I want you to
know that we support you.

171
00:10:55,988 --> 00:10:58,991
That bastard asked for whip,
but that's never gonna happen.

172
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:01,077
Not while I'm in this office.
I wanted to assure you.

173
00:11:01,869 --> 00:11:03,580
I've always tried to help where I can.

174
00:11:03,663 --> 00:11:06,082
And you couldn't help at
Health and Human Services?

175
00:11:07,083 --> 00:11:09,711
The agency does some amazing work.

176
00:11:10,628 --> 00:11:14,090
Well, you were quoted as, uh,
calling your position a dream job.

177
00:11:14,966 --> 00:11:17,134
It's possible to have
more than one dream.

178
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:20,638
I can have my assistant give you a tour.

179
00:11:20,722 --> 00:11:23,099
Uh, show you some of
the video from Haiti

180
00:11:23,182 --> 00:11:25,935
and some of the work that
we've been doing there

181
00:11:26,018 --> 00:11:27,437
over the last several months.

182
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,439
I will be sure to include

183
00:11:29,522 --> 00:11:31,107
your organization in
the piece I'm writing.

184
00:11:31,190 --> 00:11:32,932
I believe in the work
you're doing here.

185
00:11:32,957 --> 00:11:33,716
Thank you.

186
00:11:34,611 --> 00:11:37,196
What was it like, uh, working with
the Underwood administration?

187
00:11:38,573 --> 00:11:41,075
They were fine.
Let's leave it at that.

188
00:11:43,953 --> 00:11:46,498
You know, I've met with, uh, many
wonderful organizations so far.

189
00:11:46,581 --> 00:11:50,752
- The Virginia Children's Defense Fund.
- They do some great work.

190
00:11:50,835 --> 00:11:53,087
Mmm, uh, The Talbot League?

191
00:11:53,170 --> 00:11:54,422
Another terrific group.

192
00:11:56,090 --> 00:11:59,051
The Anthony Moretti Memorial Fund.

193
00:12:01,804 --> 00:12:04,223
I don't know anything about them.

194
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:07,852
Organ transplantation awareness.

195
00:12:07,935 --> 00:12:09,854
Oh, is that right?

196
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:11,981
Hmm.

197
00:12:14,984 --> 00:12:16,027
Hi.

198
00:12:17,487 --> 00:12:19,906
I'm so sorry.

199
00:12:19,989 --> 00:12:22,784
It's rude, but I was famished.

200
00:12:22,867 --> 00:12:25,412
Thank you for meeting me.
Let me get you a menu.

201
00:12:25,495 --> 00:12:28,290
Uh, no, thank you. I have
a lunch right after this.

202
00:12:29,123 --> 00:12:31,584
Good. You should
always have one on the books.

203
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:41,511
- Miss Davis?
- You're worried about Aidan.

204
00:12:42,512 --> 00:12:44,597
Do you call him anything for short?

205
00:12:46,182 --> 00:12:47,600
Why would you ask me that?

206
00:12:49,226 --> 00:12:51,438
I don't know if the
president, or vice president,

207
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,272
has informed you, but Mac is fine.

208
00:12:53,356 --> 00:12:57,444
It's what he knows is the problem,
and who he may have told it to.

209
00:12:58,695 --> 00:13:00,530
He wants to stay alive.

210
00:13:03,074 --> 00:13:04,492
Yes.

211
00:13:04,576 --> 00:13:07,036
But those are the ones you
have to watch out for.

212
00:13:09,456 --> 00:13:12,542
Last I spoke to Aidan was
minutes before he got in a cab.

213
00:13:13,125 --> 00:13:15,378
I don't know what he
has or what he's done.

214
00:13:15,462 --> 00:13:17,088
I only do what I'm told.

215
00:13:19,215 --> 00:13:20,925
I'm in the Hoover Building.

216
00:13:21,008 --> 00:13:24,095
It's the ugliest building in...
in the city.

217
00:13:24,178 --> 00:13:25,888
I close my eyes as I walk in.

218
00:13:27,306 --> 00:13:30,518
Stop by any time. Especially
if you're in the mood to talk.

219
00:13:30,602 --> 00:13:33,312
We could use someone who
says as little as you do.

220
00:14:41,839 --> 00:14:43,132
Lisa!

221
00:14:50,056 --> 00:14:52,892
- I've been looking for her.
- I have no idea where she is.

222
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:57,021
- Let me help you with that.
- No. No, I'm okay.

223
00:14:58,773 --> 00:15:01,609
- I'm happy to carry it for you.
- You're scaring me.

224
00:15:03,403 --> 00:15:05,488
I'm just worried about her. That's all.

225
00:15:06,614 --> 00:15:07,740
I'm clean.

226
00:15:08,950 --> 00:15:09,951
Good.

227
00:15:10,034 --> 00:15:13,663
At least I'm trying to get clean.
Please leave me alone.

228
00:15:13,746 --> 00:15:15,164
Hey...

229
00:15:22,839 --> 00:15:23,840
Just...

230
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:27,552
If you hear from Rachel, just, uh...

231
00:15:28,845 --> 00:15:30,805
tell her I've taken care of everything.

232
00:15:32,724 --> 00:15:34,892
She can come home and
everything will be fine.

233
00:15:41,899 --> 00:15:43,317
I miss her, you know?

234
00:15:50,533 --> 00:15:51,534
I'm sorry.

235
00:16:23,733 --> 00:16:24,901
You going to answer that?

236
00:16:24,984 --> 00:16:26,653
So, was I right?

237
00:16:26,736 --> 00:16:28,404
Your phone is ringing.

238
00:16:30,197 --> 00:16:31,448
Uh, oh...

239
00:16:37,163 --> 00:16:39,666
You know, you can just hang it up
by pressing the button on the side.

240
00:16:39,749 --> 00:16:42,334
- You don't have to decline...
- I'm a creature of habit.

241
00:16:44,837 --> 00:16:46,839
So, yeah, you were right.

242
00:16:46,923 --> 00:16:50,342
This phone has only been used
once to call one number, once.

243
00:16:50,885 --> 00:16:53,095
I mean, I used the
manufacturer's serial number

244
00:16:53,179 --> 00:16:55,097
and tracked down where
it's distributed to.

245
00:16:55,181 --> 00:16:57,850
It was a, um... a shop in Dupont Circle.

246
00:16:57,934 --> 00:16:59,185
And who did it call?

247
00:16:59,811 --> 00:17:01,395
Comes up unknown.

248
00:17:01,478 --> 00:17:03,105
But the prefix...

249
00:17:03,189 --> 00:17:05,900
And, look, you're gonna have to
talk to the police or the FBI.

250
00:17:05,983 --> 00:17:08,027
- I just can't keep...
- Don't worry. It's on me.

251
00:17:08,110 --> 00:17:11,447
The prefix on the call, it looks
like a government number to me.

252
00:17:15,284 --> 00:17:16,452
Hello.

253
00:17:19,121 --> 00:17:21,791
He followed you? When?

254
00:17:21,874 --> 00:17:24,001
- I've got to...
- Yeah.

255
00:17:25,878 --> 00:17:28,380
Okay. Okay, if you feel safe
where you are, stay there.

256
00:17:28,464 --> 00:17:29,674
I'm coming to you.

257
00:17:32,051 --> 00:17:33,094
Thanks.

258
00:17:35,346 --> 00:17:37,389
So what couldn't you
tell me over the phone?

259
00:17:38,307 --> 00:17:39,642
Jackie Sharp.

260
00:17:40,309 --> 00:17:41,603
What about her?

261
00:17:43,062 --> 00:17:44,355
You really don't know?

262
00:17:46,023 --> 00:17:48,067
I thought this was coming
out of the White House.

263
00:17:49,401 --> 00:17:53,489
The story's about to break that she
traded legislation for campaign funds.

264
00:17:53,573 --> 00:17:54,616
The source?

265
00:17:55,825 --> 00:17:57,744
So, unconfirmed.

266
00:17:58,452 --> 00:18:00,537
It's going to be on
<i>Drudge</i> within the hour.

267
00:18:02,414 --> 00:18:04,250
You really don't have anything to say?

268
00:18:05,417 --> 00:18:08,170
If it's true, it's just sad
to see the unexpected fall

269
00:18:08,254 --> 00:18:09,797
of a once rising star in
the Democratic Party.

270
00:18:09,881 --> 00:18:11,007
Stop it.

271
00:18:12,299 --> 00:18:14,468
Sharp would've been a
get for the committee.

272
00:18:16,846 --> 00:18:20,141
If you're still willing to appear,
we're still willing to talk.

273
00:18:24,561 --> 00:18:25,730
See ya.

274
00:18:34,614 --> 00:18:37,074
This is not how we do things
in this administration.

275
00:18:37,158 --> 00:18:39,035
You wanted her not to testify.

276
00:18:39,118 --> 00:18:41,037
And there's no way Romero
can force her to now.

277
00:18:43,956 --> 00:18:47,627
You've put my press secretary and his
staff in a very difficult situation.

278
00:18:47,710 --> 00:18:50,379
It was a leak, and it
didn't come from you.

279
00:18:50,462 --> 00:18:52,339
I really felt this was the
way to get ahead of it.

280
00:18:52,423 --> 00:18:53,424
This is getting ahead of it?

281
00:18:53,507 --> 00:18:56,636
I guarantee you her lawyers tell her to
plead the Fifth now, across the board.

282
00:18:56,719 --> 00:18:58,387
Most importantly, where it concerns you.

283
00:18:58,470 --> 00:19:00,973
But all of this happened while
I was negotiating with Petrov.

284
00:19:01,057 --> 00:19:02,850
Oh, to the public, you
were the First Lady,

285
00:19:02,934 --> 00:19:04,727
sitting vigil by your husband's bed.

286
00:19:04,811 --> 00:19:07,479
So, actually, all of this happened
under Donald Blythe's watch.

287
00:19:09,190 --> 00:19:10,650
I'm sorry if you think I overstepped.

288
00:19:12,443 --> 00:19:14,028
I was trying to protect you.

289
00:19:22,453 --> 00:19:23,996
He did the right thing.

290
00:19:24,080 --> 00:19:25,497
Oh, I don't mind what he did.

291
00:19:26,332 --> 00:19:27,834
I mind the way he did it.

292
00:19:29,711 --> 00:19:31,503
- LeAnn...
- Madam Vice President.

293
00:19:31,587 --> 00:19:33,464
I'm sorry my schedule is so full.

294
00:19:33,547 --> 00:19:36,217
No, I completely understand, with
everything that you're dealing with.

295
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:38,177
I'm sorry for being so insistent,

296
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:40,847
but I thought you should know that
Jane Davis came after me yesterday.

297
00:19:40,930 --> 00:19:41,973
Came after you?

298
00:19:42,056 --> 00:19:44,684
She wanted me to work for her.
I didn't know if you knew.

299
00:19:44,767 --> 00:19:45,893
In what capacity?

300
00:19:46,811 --> 00:19:49,105
That, I'm not clear on, but it...

301
00:19:49,188 --> 00:19:51,899
She wanted information
about Aidan Macallan.

302
00:19:51,983 --> 00:19:54,026
I hope you know I would
never say a word.

303
00:19:55,361 --> 00:19:57,321
Thank you for bringing
this to my attention.

304
00:19:58,740 --> 00:20:01,743
Ma'am, I was hoping we
could discuss my future.

305
00:20:01,826 --> 00:20:05,079
Oh, LeAnn, I would love to discuss
your future, but not yet.

306
00:20:06,998 --> 00:20:10,251
If you would like, ma'am,
I can help with Aidan.

307
00:20:10,334 --> 00:20:11,460
Anything that's required.

308
00:20:12,211 --> 00:20:13,504
That's good to know.

309
00:20:33,565 --> 00:20:36,568
Did you think LeAnn
Harvey wouldn't tell me?

310
00:20:36,652 --> 00:20:39,196
I never assume what people
will do or won't do.

311
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:41,319
I thought we were
working well together.

312
00:20:41,344 --> 00:20:42,052
We are.

313
00:20:42,199 --> 00:20:47,454
Well, going around me to get information
will only create discord between us.

314
00:20:48,039 --> 00:20:49,832
I wouldn't want that, Claire.

315
00:20:50,917 --> 00:20:52,794
- Miss Davis.
- Mr. President.

316
00:20:52,877 --> 00:20:54,086
I'm sorry I'm late.

317
00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:56,130
The vice president has
told me that your contacts

318
00:20:56,213 --> 00:20:58,758
have once again lost track of Ahmadi.

319
00:20:58,841 --> 00:21:00,259
Yes, as I explained,

320
00:21:00,342 --> 00:21:03,512
Syria is like a different
country one day to the next.

321
00:21:03,595 --> 00:21:05,765
Yes, well, you can thank
Viktor Petrov for that.

322
00:21:05,848 --> 00:21:07,516
Oh, I do.

323
00:21:10,144 --> 00:21:11,187
Ma'am?

324
00:21:11,270 --> 00:21:13,230
Oh, you'll have to excuse me, Jane.

325
00:21:13,314 --> 00:21:15,399
The schedule is crazy these days.

326
00:21:15,482 --> 00:21:16,776
Oh, of course.

327
00:21:19,028 --> 00:21:21,197
Really? The State
Department, the Pentagon,

328
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:22,907
we can't get close to Ahmadi?

329
00:21:22,990 --> 00:21:24,658
I just want peace.

330
00:21:24,741 --> 00:21:27,244
Yes, there's nothing that
makes profits like peace.

331
00:21:27,328 --> 00:21:30,497
And nothing makes peace like profits.

332
00:21:31,707 --> 00:21:34,293
Mr. President, may I be blunt?

333
00:21:35,419 --> 00:21:38,130
Well, I would never accuse
you of dullness, Miss Davis.

334
00:21:41,092 --> 00:21:45,179
There's going to be a gas attack
happening in the city of Homs.

335
00:21:46,889 --> 00:21:49,766
If you were to, let us say,
draw a line in the sand...

336
00:21:50,476 --> 00:21:53,104
and if this tragic
attack were to happen...

337
00:21:54,105 --> 00:21:59,610
the US government would have to send in
an additional 30,000 or so soldiers.

338
00:22:00,194 --> 00:22:05,157
To protect the oil lines, to keep
Petrov from getting into Damascus.

339
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:08,660
And, of course, to protect the people.

340
00:22:10,830 --> 00:22:14,834
That's venturing close to occupation
with those kind of numbers.

341
00:22:14,917 --> 00:22:19,088
All I'm saying is maybe the
Declaration of War Committee

342
00:22:19,171 --> 00:22:21,715
could finally debate what
they were mandated to.

343
00:22:22,633 --> 00:22:23,634
A war.

344
00:22:30,224 --> 00:22:32,101
Do you know Mark Usher?

345
00:22:35,646 --> 00:22:38,815
Yes, we worked together.

346
00:22:39,483 --> 00:22:41,652
He was handling a congressman.

347
00:22:42,319 --> 00:22:43,404
Mark.

348
00:22:45,239 --> 00:22:46,657
Has anyone offered you anything?

349
00:22:46,740 --> 00:22:48,284
Oh, no, I'm good. Thanks.

350
00:22:58,085 --> 00:23:00,796
Have you ever worked with Jane Davis?

351
00:23:02,798 --> 00:23:05,051
Davis is the stepfather's name.

352
00:23:06,260 --> 00:23:07,719
A one-term senator.

353
00:23:09,305 --> 00:23:13,600
Uh, anyway, yeah, she was interested
in a client I had, at some point.

354
00:23:14,726 --> 00:23:16,228
A candidate for Congress.

355
00:23:16,312 --> 00:23:20,149
Poor guy. The minute Usher realized
he wasn't formidable enough,

356
00:23:20,232 --> 00:23:22,193
he just dumped him. Let him go.

357
00:23:23,277 --> 00:23:24,278
But that's Mark.

358
00:23:25,737 --> 00:23:27,698
He has an eye for talent.

359
00:23:27,781 --> 00:23:30,867
Likes to keep you close while
he keeps himself remote.

360
00:23:31,368 --> 00:23:34,580
But he disagreed with
some of her positions,

361
00:23:34,663 --> 00:23:35,872
the money went away.

362
00:23:36,790 --> 00:23:40,419
Listen, don't let Jane fool you.

363
00:23:41,795 --> 00:23:45,549
She can seem a bit... haphazard...

364
00:23:45,632 --> 00:23:47,301
<i>But once he's done with you...</i>

365
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:52,181
it's as if a switch gets turned off.

366
00:23:52,264 --> 00:23:53,515
<i>But she is merciless.</i>

367
00:23:55,892 --> 00:23:58,020
Single-minded as a single mind can be.

368
00:24:05,236 --> 00:24:06,320
Mmm.

369
00:24:13,535 --> 00:24:14,620
Mmm.

370
00:24:16,288 --> 00:24:19,291
What? Forgot to eat today.

371
00:24:20,376 --> 00:24:21,752
What have you got for me?

372
00:24:24,296 --> 00:24:26,882
- I don't know.
- Don't do "I don't know."

373
00:24:29,260 --> 00:24:30,677
Saw Meredith Lee.

374
00:24:32,096 --> 00:24:36,392
I mentioned the Moretti Fund,
organ transplants, uh...

375
00:24:36,475 --> 00:24:39,020
She got a little tense, but...
I don't know.

376
00:24:40,687 --> 00:24:41,980
Why didn't you tell me you were fired?

377
00:24:42,564 --> 00:24:44,941
- Oh, that was you?
- Who talked to your girlfriend? Yeah.

378
00:24:46,068 --> 00:24:47,153
How long?

379
00:24:48,112 --> 00:24:50,239
What's the difference? I'm tapped.

380
00:24:52,741 --> 00:24:55,536
What are we doing here? I
don't like eating in my car.

381
00:24:58,414 --> 00:25:01,083
I think Laura Moretti's husband
was jumped on the donor list.

382
00:25:02,959 --> 00:25:05,129
- Where the hell did you get that?
- My gut.

383
00:25:06,047 --> 00:25:07,173
I can lay it out for you.

384
00:25:07,256 --> 00:25:09,550
Yeah, you say something
like that, you better.

385
00:25:10,384 --> 00:25:14,513
Sarin gas. Mostly against civilians
in suburban areas of Homs.

386
00:25:14,596 --> 00:25:17,308
- We're dealing with animals here.
- It's horrifying.

387
00:25:17,391 --> 00:25:20,186
Now, you know I've always shown
restraint in the past, but...

388
00:25:20,269 --> 00:25:24,523
I'm going to be making an executive
order for military action.

389
00:25:24,606 --> 00:25:27,651
Air strikes followed by ground troops.

390
00:25:27,734 --> 00:25:29,570
You'll need to present to the UN.

391
00:25:30,404 --> 00:25:32,802
Where is this intelligence coming from?

392
00:25:32,886 --> 00:25:34,810
Well, it's all there in the briefing.

393
00:25:35,159 --> 00:25:37,203
But if we know this is going to happen,

394
00:25:37,286 --> 00:25:40,956
well, then why aren't we
stopping it before it does?

395
00:25:45,586 --> 00:25:48,505
Francis held on to your
resignation letter.

396
00:25:49,965 --> 00:25:52,301
And he wants to tear it up.

397
00:25:59,683 --> 00:26:04,146
I know how difficult these past
few months have been for you.

398
00:26:04,230 --> 00:26:06,357
We've really asked a lot.

399
00:26:06,440 --> 00:26:08,567
Once we're on the other side
of this military action,

400
00:26:08,650 --> 00:26:12,363
you have our blessing to leave the
administration if you choose.

401
00:26:13,530 --> 00:26:15,741
But, of course,
we'd prefer if you stay on.

402
00:26:16,450 --> 00:26:18,327
Hope that goes without saying.

403
00:26:23,624 --> 00:26:27,669
Using weapons of mass destruction
against civilians is a war crime.

404
00:26:27,753 --> 00:26:31,215
I appreciate the opportunity to
see this through to the end.

405
00:26:31,298 --> 00:26:33,467
We were hoping you would say that.

406
00:26:33,550 --> 00:26:34,718
Thank you, Cathy.

407
00:26:34,801 --> 00:26:36,303
Thank you, Mr. President.

408
00:26:41,642 --> 00:26:43,185
Are you worried about her?

409
00:26:44,603 --> 00:26:48,190
We need to start thinking about the
people we surround ourselves with.

410
00:26:49,107 --> 00:26:51,360
Which has made me think of Tom...

411
00:26:52,569 --> 00:26:57,199
who doesn't really have a reason
to be here anymore, does he?

412
00:26:59,410 --> 00:27:00,744
Not an official one, no,

413
00:27:00,827 --> 00:27:03,872
but I thought maybe we could
bring him on as a consultant.

414
00:27:14,883 --> 00:27:17,052
Well, of course, that's
entirely up to you.

415
00:27:18,179 --> 00:27:21,139
There's just only so much time
and energy in a single day,

416
00:27:21,223 --> 00:27:23,725
and I think we need to
constantly be asking ourselves,

417
00:27:23,809 --> 00:27:26,895
"Am I spending it as wisely as I can?"

418
00:27:39,866 --> 00:27:41,785
- Thank you.
- Jane, I'm so sorry

419
00:27:41,868 --> 00:27:43,370
- to pull you away from your party.
- No, no, no.

420
00:27:43,454 --> 00:27:44,979
When I heard your voice on the phone...

421
00:27:45,004 --> 00:27:45,980
I just... I just...

422
00:27:46,039 --> 00:27:49,042
- I don't know what to do.
- Did something happen?

423
00:27:49,125 --> 00:27:53,046
The Underwoods are planning air
strikes and a troop surge in Homs.

424
00:27:55,299 --> 00:27:58,260
- How awful.
- Do you know anything about this?

425
00:28:00,221 --> 00:28:04,140
Petrov is making the
whole region a disaster.

426
00:28:04,225 --> 00:28:07,478
No one who ever got tangled up in
the Middle East came out ahead.

427
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:09,896
Let Petrov own it.

428
00:28:14,860 --> 00:28:17,946
There's something terrible
that is going to happen...

429
00:28:18,029 --> 00:28:19,448
that we can prevent.

430
00:28:19,531 --> 00:28:20,616
I see.

431
00:28:21,157 --> 00:28:24,119
I'm scheduled to present some
kind of red line at the UN.

432
00:28:24,202 --> 00:28:26,747
- But we can still pull back.
- How can I help?

433
00:28:27,623 --> 00:28:30,751
Talk to the president,
the vice president.

434
00:28:32,794 --> 00:28:34,087
I will.

435
00:28:36,757 --> 00:28:39,468
They're terrible, terrible
people, aren't they?

436
00:28:43,805 --> 00:28:44,806
Jane...

437
00:28:46,517 --> 00:28:48,394
be very careful with them.

438
00:28:50,687 --> 00:28:51,688
Of course.

439
00:28:54,275 --> 00:28:56,026
That's from an old friend.

440
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:00,113
Are we still friends?

441
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:02,908
Of course.

442
00:29:24,179 --> 00:29:26,014
He says the liver the president received

443
00:29:26,097 --> 00:29:29,518
was supposed to go to a man
named Anthony Moretti.

444
00:29:29,601 --> 00:29:31,227
And he says that Doug forced

445
00:29:31,312 --> 00:29:33,439
then Secretary of Health
and Human Services

446
00:29:33,522 --> 00:29:35,190
to put the president at
the top of the list,

447
00:29:35,274 --> 00:29:36,984
which resulted in Moretti's death.

448
00:29:38,277 --> 00:29:42,531
Oh, and Doug also has become
involved with the man's widow.

449
00:29:43,114 --> 00:29:44,199
That's a nice touch.

450
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:47,494
And does... does he have proof?

451
00:29:47,578 --> 00:29:50,331
About the relationship?
It's easy to prove that.

452
00:29:51,164 --> 00:29:53,292
But the list, he knows that
Meredith Lee is hiding something.

453
00:29:53,375 --> 00:29:55,919
And he's ready to go back
and talk to her about it.

454
00:29:56,837 --> 00:29:57,963
Do you trust him?

455
00:29:59,381 --> 00:30:01,216
I don't know why he'd
make any of this up.

456
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:03,344
Hey, I'm outside.

457
00:30:09,099 --> 00:30:10,684
Did you have a good time?

458
00:30:13,228 --> 00:30:15,439
I never do at those things anymore.

459
00:30:16,022 --> 00:30:17,608
Necessary evils.

460
00:30:17,691 --> 00:30:21,236
It's not like the old days, when you
used to meet surprising people.

461
00:30:22,654 --> 00:30:26,241
Uh... did people get less surprising
or are we less easily surprised?

462
00:30:28,243 --> 00:30:29,828
Well, exactly.

463
00:30:30,579 --> 00:30:32,539
- This is me.
- She asked me about you.

464
00:30:34,833 --> 00:30:37,336
- I figured. What'd you say?
- That you're...

465
00:30:39,755 --> 00:30:42,674
formidable, etcetera.

466
00:30:43,759 --> 00:30:48,430
Yeah, I told him the same
about you, etcetera.

467
00:30:50,641 --> 00:30:52,183
He could be a great president.

468
00:30:54,978 --> 00:30:56,312
But does he want to be?

469
00:30:56,397 --> 00:30:58,649
And what is great?

470
00:31:01,067 --> 00:31:02,611
And then there's her.

471
00:31:03,695 --> 00:31:04,738
What about her?

472
00:31:06,532 --> 00:31:07,574
What about her?

473
00:31:15,916 --> 00:31:18,209
Because if Bob or Terry
were the ranking member,

474
00:31:18,293 --> 00:31:19,545
I'd be talking to them.

475
00:31:20,462 --> 00:31:23,214
I want Romero's clerk stripped.

476
00:31:23,799 --> 00:31:26,468
Yeah, whatever budget he's got
for his seat on that committee.

477
00:31:26,552 --> 00:31:30,722
No, no, I'm asking for
the sake of the party.

478
00:31:30,806 --> 00:31:33,475
Look, we survived Jackie Sharp,
but it's not gonna end there.

479
00:31:34,393 --> 00:31:36,562
The truth is I'm being punished

480
00:31:36,645 --> 00:31:39,856
for fighting for the issues
that our base cares most about.

481
00:31:41,400 --> 00:31:43,860
Yes, and thank you.

482
00:31:46,321 --> 00:31:48,740
Doyle says there's
almost nothing on Usher.

483
00:31:49,240 --> 00:31:51,993
He likes to gamble, usually wins.

484
00:31:52,077 --> 00:31:54,830
But here's the thing...
Doyle spoke with Marshall,

485
00:31:54,913 --> 00:31:58,291
who we know did not have a happy
exit from the Conway campaign.

486
00:31:58,374 --> 00:32:01,503
Now, he said that before Usher
joined our administration,

487
00:32:01,587 --> 00:32:04,339
he was grooming Romero
for a presidential run.

488
00:32:06,467 --> 00:32:10,386
Sir, I believe he's playing both sides
of whatever this committee is after.

489
00:32:11,763 --> 00:32:14,725
I'm not
a Democrat, Jeffrey.

490
00:32:14,808 --> 00:32:18,019
I'm still a Republican, just happen
to work for the White House.

491
00:32:18,103 --> 00:32:21,106
All I'm saying is let's rein
Alex in while we still can.

492
00:32:21,982 --> 00:32:23,525
'Cause after he rips
through his own party,

493
00:32:23,608 --> 00:32:25,068
he'll come after ours,
wouldn't you think?

494
00:32:25,819 --> 00:32:28,697
Yeah, and Jeff, you're the chair...
No... What? No, listen.

495
00:32:28,780 --> 00:32:30,824
Wait. Wait! Wait.

496
00:32:32,408 --> 00:32:34,536
And you owe me, and we both know it,

497
00:32:34,620 --> 00:32:36,872
and that's not the kind of
thing I like to say out loud.

498
00:32:36,955 --> 00:32:37,956
What is?

499
00:32:40,125 --> 00:32:41,502
When did that happen?

500
00:32:44,588 --> 00:32:45,797
Confirmed?

501
00:32:52,513 --> 00:32:53,597
Yeah?

502
00:32:54,556 --> 00:32:55,766
Yes, well...

503
00:32:57,643 --> 00:32:59,144
just because Walker's
gonna take the stand,

504
00:32:59,227 --> 00:33:01,312
doesn't mean he'll say anything. Right?

505
00:33:02,814 --> 00:33:03,982
Hmm.

506
00:33:14,159 --> 00:33:16,452
What was that word you
said to me this morning?

507
00:33:17,370 --> 00:33:19,164
The one no one uses anymore?

508
00:33:20,081 --> 00:33:21,082
Crepuscular.

509
00:33:22,167 --> 00:33:23,251
Right.

510
00:33:27,589 --> 00:33:31,176
Some of my favorite
animals are crepuscular.

511
00:33:33,595 --> 00:33:34,805
Yes.

512
00:33:35,722 --> 00:33:36,723
What?

513
00:33:37,808 --> 00:33:38,809
When?

514
00:33:39,726 --> 00:33:41,895
Jesus! God damn it!

515
00:33:49,736 --> 00:33:50,946
Claire!

516
00:33:52,072 --> 00:33:54,199
- Claire!
- I'm in the kitchen.

517
00:33:55,701 --> 00:33:57,619
Walker's gonna testify.

518
00:33:57,703 --> 00:33:59,705
- When?
- Early as next week.

519
00:34:00,706 --> 00:34:02,749
He's gonna say the same
thing he said to Hammerschmidt.

520
00:34:02,832 --> 00:34:04,084
There's not gonna be any new damage.

521
00:34:04,167 --> 00:34:06,127
- We don't know that for sure.
- Let's get Usher.

522
00:34:06,211 --> 00:34:08,171
You bet I'm gonna
talk to him in the morning.

523
00:34:08,254 --> 00:34:09,297
Where are you going?

524
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:12,133
To get some goddamn space in this house!

525
00:34:28,650 --> 00:34:31,737
I can take care of Walker.
We'll make him an offer.

526
00:34:32,654 --> 00:34:34,072
A place back on the stage,

527
00:34:34,155 --> 00:34:36,074
chancellor of some well-endowed
university. He'll take it.

528
00:34:36,157 --> 00:34:39,369
He just might. But first, I want
to know what you have on Romero.

529
00:34:39,995 --> 00:34:41,287
I don't have a thing.

530
00:34:41,371 --> 00:34:43,957
- Please.
- Doug, let's not be rude.

531
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:45,876
I find that impossible to believe.

532
00:34:45,959 --> 00:34:47,127
If I did, you would know it.

533
00:34:47,210 --> 00:34:48,879
You and I have been at this a long time.

534
00:34:48,962 --> 00:34:51,757
You wouldn't even consider a
candidate without vetting him first.

535
00:34:51,840 --> 00:34:53,008
I thought Alex had a future.

536
00:34:53,091 --> 00:34:55,010
Yeah, a run for the presidency
as I understand it.

537
00:34:55,093 --> 00:34:57,428
He's clean and crisp as
a starched white shirt.

538
00:34:57,512 --> 00:34:58,805
You're gonna have to
make a decision, Mark.

539
00:34:58,889 --> 00:35:00,807
You wanna work for me
and Claire, or not?

540
00:35:00,891 --> 00:35:04,310
I have already reached out
to members of my own party.

541
00:35:04,394 --> 00:35:06,897
I've used my political
capital to help you.

542
00:35:06,980 --> 00:35:08,356
But you're still not
answering my question.

543
00:35:08,439 --> 00:35:09,775
I think I have.

544
00:35:10,817 --> 00:35:13,528
And I don't need to convince you or Mrs.
Underwood of my worth.

545
00:35:23,747 --> 00:35:25,206
It's an honor to meet you.

546
00:35:26,416 --> 00:35:29,585
Seth, Mr. Jeffries will catch
up with you momentarily.

547
00:35:33,256 --> 00:35:34,883
I'm going to keep this brief.

548
00:35:38,469 --> 00:35:40,596
If what you're saying is true...

549
00:35:40,681 --> 00:35:44,017
Doug Stamper saved my husband's life.

550
00:35:44,100 --> 00:35:45,185
He did.

551
00:35:45,852 --> 00:35:48,146
And he also cost someone else his.

552
00:35:49,981 --> 00:35:54,820
Well, I suspect you're the kind
of man that wants many things.

553
00:35:55,737 --> 00:35:57,197
But you have made a mistake.

554
00:35:58,990 --> 00:36:02,661
There's only one thing, professionally,
that I've always wanted.

555
00:36:03,912 --> 00:36:06,790
- It's to work in the White House.
- Hmm.

556
00:36:06,873 --> 00:36:08,291
Seth will see you out.

557
00:36:12,879 --> 00:36:14,505
Sorry for the interruption.

558
00:36:17,342 --> 00:36:18,468
You were saying...

559
00:36:21,221 --> 00:36:24,099
Cathy was always a very good person.

560
00:36:24,933 --> 00:36:27,060
At her core, she has a core.

561
00:36:27,143 --> 00:36:28,228
Bless her.

562
00:36:28,812 --> 00:36:33,191
This whole Homs initiative might
be too difficult for her.

563
00:36:36,486 --> 00:36:40,490
You know... you're very hard to read.

564
00:36:41,908 --> 00:36:43,243
Which makes sense.

565
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:47,789
For the last several years,
you've been a familiar face.

566
00:36:49,582 --> 00:36:51,626
Must get exhausting.

567
00:36:51,710 --> 00:36:55,088
Must be hardly any people you
can even share a laugh with.

568
00:37:02,303 --> 00:37:05,431
You should figure out how to
take full advantage of me.

569
00:37:07,100 --> 00:37:08,894
I can help you get what you want.

570
00:37:10,771 --> 00:37:12,272
If you tell me what it is.

571
00:37:12,355 --> 00:37:14,190
I enjoy helping others.

572
00:37:15,400 --> 00:37:16,943
I think you know what I want.

573
00:37:17,027 --> 00:37:18,194
Yes...

574
00:37:19,279 --> 00:37:21,740
But do you want it now or in four years?

575
00:37:27,871 --> 00:37:32,708
I want the whole world to
see Ahmadi's capture...

576
00:37:32,793 --> 00:37:35,921
on every news outlet and every screen.

577
00:37:37,172 --> 00:37:39,715
For the good of the party, I'd
prefer Mr. Walker not testify.

578
00:37:40,300 --> 00:37:43,178
I'm considering taking myself off
the Declaration of War Committee.

579
00:37:43,261 --> 00:37:46,431
- Alex, you don't have to do that.
- And you can do what you will with it.

580
00:37:47,265 --> 00:37:50,310
But that's conditional on the
president doing the right thing.

581
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:53,814
Uh-huh. And what is that?

582
00:37:53,897 --> 00:37:55,065
Resigning.

583
00:37:55,148 --> 00:37:57,358
Again, I'm only thinking of the party.

584
00:38:01,070 --> 00:38:04,240
Yeah...

585
00:38:06,576 --> 00:38:09,079
Who the fuck do you think you are?

586
00:38:09,162 --> 00:38:11,915
- Oh, don't use that language with me...
- Shut the fuck up.

587
00:38:13,041 --> 00:38:15,460
You have no idea, none,
about what you're doing.

588
00:38:15,543 --> 00:38:18,046
- I'd like you to leave my office.
- Now you listen carefully to me.

589
00:38:18,129 --> 00:38:19,464
- I said...
- Shut up, Alex!

590
00:38:21,466 --> 00:38:22,633
Here's a headline for you.

591
00:38:22,717 --> 00:38:25,136
"Alex Romero colluded
with Governor Conway

592
00:38:25,220 --> 00:38:28,473
to use the Declaration of War Committee
to help him win an election."

593
00:38:28,556 --> 00:38:31,434
- No.
- Please, you know you did.

594
00:38:32,768 --> 00:38:34,687
- See how easy that is?
- Get out!

595
00:38:36,772 --> 00:38:40,193
You're gonna be all alone at your
hearing. I just left Walker.

596
00:38:40,276 --> 00:38:42,445
He's gonna plead the Fifth.
You'll look like a fool.

597
00:38:44,072 --> 00:38:47,117
You want to be out front? No
one has a problem with that.

598
00:38:48,034 --> 00:38:51,496
They want you there, because
you'll be the first to fall.

599
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:56,501
"For the good of the party."

600
00:39:03,258 --> 00:39:04,843
Sleep tight, Congressman.

601
00:39:04,926 --> 00:39:08,138
- Who knows about Will?
- No one, not yet.

602
00:39:08,221 --> 00:39:09,389
What else?

603
00:39:11,474 --> 00:39:13,684
Why I'm still protecting
you at this point...

604
00:39:14,310 --> 00:39:16,104
I don't even understand myself.

605
00:39:21,192 --> 00:39:23,028
<i>Walker's gonna plead the Fifth.</i>

606
00:39:23,111 --> 00:39:24,779
<i>And Romero won't rock the boat.</i>

607
00:39:24,863 --> 00:39:26,697
Thank you, Mark, I appreciate that.

608
00:39:26,781 --> 00:39:29,075
<i>I would suggest you
schedule a press conference</i>

609
00:39:29,159 --> 00:39:30,618
<i>for right after the hearing.</i>

610
00:39:30,701 --> 00:39:31,702
All right.

611
00:39:46,968 --> 00:39:49,137
Tell me the worst thing
you've ever done.

612
00:39:52,182 --> 00:39:53,474
It's hard to pick.

613
00:39:54,392 --> 00:39:56,644
- I'm serious.
- So am I.

614
00:40:03,068 --> 00:40:05,904
Pretended to love someone when I didn't.

615
00:40:08,114 --> 00:40:09,740
In a way, that was the worst.

616
00:40:16,998 --> 00:40:18,499
Did the person know?

617
00:40:20,168 --> 00:40:21,252
Mmm-hmm.

618
00:40:22,628 --> 00:40:24,422
She pretended not to know...

619
00:40:26,091 --> 00:40:27,425
but she knew.

620
00:40:28,676 --> 00:40:30,220
It was awful for her.

621
00:40:33,181 --> 00:40:35,266
But I paid for that mistake, too.

622
00:40:37,768 --> 00:40:39,770
Those types of things, they take a toll.

623
00:40:41,147 --> 00:40:43,944
It's strange how some
choices mean nothing

624
00:40:44,391 --> 00:40:47,057
and others govern the
rest of your life.

625
00:40:49,530 --> 00:40:51,407
I've done some terrible things.

626
00:40:54,619 --> 00:40:56,037
You don't have to tell me.

627
00:40:58,831 --> 00:41:00,666
Francis killed Zoe Barnes.

628
00:41:02,127 --> 00:41:03,128
Claire.

629
00:41:03,211 --> 00:41:04,629
Francis killed Peter.

630
00:41:04,712 --> 00:41:05,838
Stop.

631
00:41:05,921 --> 00:41:07,590
And so many more are gonna die.

632
00:41:07,673 --> 00:41:08,716
Stop.

633
00:41:23,731 --> 00:41:25,150
You don't scare me.

634
00:41:26,692 --> 00:41:27,902
I should.

635
00:41:42,417 --> 00:41:44,002
Aidan.

636
00:41:44,085 --> 00:41:46,004
Shh! Not anymore.

637
00:41:46,087 --> 00:41:47,422
How did you get here?

638
00:41:47,505 --> 00:41:50,008
A door opened, I walked out.

639
00:41:50,091 --> 00:41:51,426
That doesn't sound right.

640
00:41:51,509 --> 00:41:54,345
I got a visa and a flight tonight.
I just...

641
00:41:55,846 --> 00:41:57,348
I just wanted to see you one last time.

642
00:41:57,432 --> 00:41:59,267
Wait. Who's getting you out?

643
00:41:59,350 --> 00:42:00,768
That doesn't matter.

644
00:42:01,894 --> 00:42:04,939
- What did you say?
- Honestly, nothing.

645
00:42:05,523 --> 00:42:06,899
We have to talk to the Underwoods.

646
00:42:06,982 --> 00:42:09,444
- Nah, I don't think so.
- This does not make sense.

647
00:42:13,948 --> 00:42:16,159
Well, you always liked
to drive a nice car.

648
00:42:19,079 --> 00:42:21,997
Aidan, come with me. I'll fix this.

649
00:42:22,082 --> 00:42:25,000
You come with me. I've a
way for you to get out.

650
00:42:32,133 --> 00:42:33,676
Well, all right, then.

651
00:42:43,644 --> 00:42:45,855
Now, don't you tell anybody
you saw me until the morning.

652
00:42:45,938 --> 00:42:48,191
Why would I tell anyone
I saw you at all?

653
00:42:48,274 --> 00:42:49,275
Really?

654
00:42:52,445 --> 00:42:53,446
Wait.

655
00:43:01,079 --> 00:43:03,164
- Here, take this.
- No.

656
00:43:03,248 --> 00:43:06,000
Come on. At least until you
get on the flight, all right?

657
00:43:10,755 --> 00:43:11,922
I'm out.

658
00:43:44,497 --> 00:43:45,831
Can't sleep?

659
00:43:48,293 --> 00:43:50,920
It's freezing out here. You
should have a coat on.

660
00:43:51,003 --> 00:43:52,087
I like it.

661
00:43:56,301 --> 00:43:57,718
Don't meet with Walker.

662
00:43:58,303 --> 00:43:59,845
I think it's a bad idea.

663
00:44:02,056 --> 00:44:04,809
It'll remind him of his
best course of action.

664
00:44:06,561 --> 00:44:09,522
You're wrong. I'm sorry,
but you're wrong.

665
00:44:10,690 --> 00:44:12,858
Usher has it under control.

666
00:44:21,826 --> 00:44:23,744
You always do what you want.

667
00:44:27,332 --> 00:44:28,749
Tom doesn't want to leave.

668
00:44:30,501 --> 00:44:32,295
Tom doesn't get to choose.

669
00:44:33,504 --> 00:44:35,089
It's cold. You should go in.

670
00:44:44,139 --> 00:44:47,977
If you could get me Doug Stamper's
addresses for the last three years...

671
00:44:48,811 --> 00:44:49,812
Uh...

672
00:44:55,443 --> 00:44:58,738
And do you know what happened to the
photo strip of Rachel and Lisa?

673
00:44:59,530 --> 00:45:00,656
It's gone.

674
00:45:01,907 --> 00:45:04,535
<i>We need to lay out
our positions ourselves.</i>

675
00:45:04,619 --> 00:45:07,037
I'm already on my way to New York.

676
00:45:07,121 --> 00:45:10,165
<i>I don't want you to say or do anything
that makes you feel uncomfortable.</i>

677
00:45:10,250 --> 00:45:13,127
I won't contradict the administration.
That's not my wish.

678
00:45:13,210 --> 00:45:15,212
We need to draw a line in the sand.

679
00:45:15,296 --> 00:45:19,174
<i>Yes, of course. Should
I get on my flight?</i>

680
00:45:19,259 --> 00:45:22,720
I think it might be best if you
turn around and come back.

681
00:45:22,803 --> 00:45:24,179
<i>Whatever you think.</i>

682
00:45:25,097 --> 00:45:26,849
We'll let Walker plead the Fifth.

683
00:45:26,932 --> 00:45:30,060
Then we'll regroup and make
sure we're on the same page.

684
00:45:31,979 --> 00:45:34,565
Okay. We need to head back.

685
00:45:45,493 --> 00:45:48,829
"Beware entangling alliances," Mr.
President.

686
00:45:49,830 --> 00:45:51,957
You know, that quote is often
attributed to Washington,

687
00:45:52,041 --> 00:45:54,084
but it was actually
Jefferson who said it.

688
00:45:56,128 --> 00:46:00,300
Anyway, I wanted to catch you before,
uh, Mark and your lawyer got here.

689
00:46:00,383 --> 00:46:01,592
Mr. President.

690
00:46:02,593 --> 00:46:04,845
I know this hasn't been easy for you.

691
00:46:06,221 --> 00:46:08,849
Well, the truth is, one can
get used to almost anything.

692
00:46:10,685 --> 00:46:13,103
Well, at any rate, congratulations.

693
00:46:13,187 --> 00:46:14,188
Oh?

694
00:46:14,272 --> 00:46:17,441
You're about to become the chancellor
of one of our finest universities.

695
00:46:17,525 --> 00:46:19,694
It's a high price we pay
for respectability.

696
00:46:21,278 --> 00:46:23,280
Anyway, I just wanted to say hello.

697
00:46:24,073 --> 00:46:26,409
And to thank you for all you're doing.

698
00:46:27,327 --> 00:46:30,871
In not too long, you're going to
become one of those ex-presidents

699
00:46:30,955 --> 00:46:36,210
whose work will make everyone...
forget the past.

700
00:46:37,628 --> 00:46:40,673
I was once the President
of the United States.

701
00:46:40,756 --> 00:46:42,299
Yes, you were.

702
00:46:42,383 --> 00:46:45,553
You stole the presidency, Frank.

703
00:46:45,636 --> 00:46:48,055
Now, Garrett...

704
00:46:48,138 --> 00:46:51,642
This is ancient history, but
by modern political standards,

705
00:46:51,726 --> 00:46:53,603
my election was a landslide.

706
00:46:54,687 --> 00:46:56,397
I was gonna change this country.

707
00:46:57,314 --> 00:47:00,693
But then... uh, things happen.

708
00:47:00,776 --> 00:47:02,194
People lie...

709
00:47:03,320 --> 00:47:04,655
You lie to keep up.

710
00:47:05,656 --> 00:47:09,034
The things that you've been
lauded for all your life,

711
00:47:09,118 --> 00:47:12,037
those are suddenly fatal flaws.

712
00:47:12,955 --> 00:47:15,791
People who've been with you
through thick and thin,

713
00:47:15,875 --> 00:47:19,420
who you thought would never
fail you, they fail you.

714
00:47:20,796 --> 00:47:24,008
You make strange alliances
with unsavory people.

715
00:47:25,593 --> 00:47:28,053
And it all becomes clear way too late.

716
00:47:31,098 --> 00:47:32,975
And no one else truly understands.

717
00:47:34,602 --> 00:47:36,729
Except someone who's
been in the office...

718
00:47:39,273 --> 00:47:43,193
You can't expect the office to do
anything more than what you bring to it.

719
00:47:43,277 --> 00:47:44,361
Hmm.

720
00:47:45,780 --> 00:47:48,824
- I'm doing this for the party.
- I know.

721
00:47:48,908 --> 00:47:50,325
And it's admirable.

722
00:47:58,125 --> 00:47:59,126
How's Tricia?

723
00:48:00,545 --> 00:48:03,005
Well, it's taken time, but we are good.

724
00:48:03,088 --> 00:48:04,590
The kids are doing well.

725
00:48:05,758 --> 00:48:07,342
Well, this is just the beginning.

726
00:48:07,968 --> 00:48:10,930
You and I are gonna be working a
lot more together from now on.

727
00:48:11,013 --> 00:48:12,097
Is that so?

728
00:48:29,490 --> 00:48:30,700
Doug.

729
00:48:33,202 --> 00:48:36,038
Tom, it's a busy morning. I don't
have any quotes right now.

730
00:48:36,121 --> 00:48:37,665
When you have a moment,

731
00:48:37,748 --> 00:48:40,709
I'd like to sit down and ask you
some questions about Lisa Williams.

732
00:48:42,503 --> 00:48:43,838
I'm sorry. What are you talking about?

733
00:48:45,172 --> 00:48:46,674
Let me know when you're free, Doug.

734
00:48:47,174 --> 00:48:48,425
If you'd like a statement,

735
00:48:48,509 --> 00:48:51,679
Seth Grayson's office will be
issuing a press release shortly.

736
00:48:51,762 --> 00:48:54,264
You live near Dupont Circle, don't you?

737
00:48:56,809 --> 00:48:59,144
I will go on the record
and say yes, I do.

738
00:49:22,459 --> 00:49:24,253
<i>President Walker,</i>

739
00:49:24,336 --> 00:49:27,757
<i>it's been established in Heather
Dunbar's special prosecutor's report</i>

740
00:49:27,840 --> 00:49:28,966
<i>that we have reason to...</i>

741
00:49:29,049 --> 00:49:31,886
<i>Congressman, as I mentioned to the chair</i>

742
00:49:31,969 --> 00:49:34,221
<i>and the other distinguished
members of the committee,</i>

743
00:49:34,304 --> 00:49:37,933
I am advising my client to
plead the Fifth at this time

744
00:49:38,017 --> 00:49:39,769
for all questions regarding his tenure

745
00:49:39,852 --> 00:49:42,271
as President of the
United States of America.

746
00:49:45,357 --> 00:49:49,987
I must say, uh, that as a fellow
Democrat, this is highly irregular.

747
00:49:50,070 --> 00:49:53,949
Yes, Congressman, yes.

748
00:49:54,033 --> 00:49:55,450
But in light of ongoing
investigations...

749
00:49:55,535 --> 00:49:58,078
Actually, I will answer the
congressman's questions.

750
00:50:03,250 --> 00:50:05,335
Please, ask your question.

751
00:50:08,422 --> 00:50:09,715
<i>Under your administration,</i>

752
00:50:09,799 --> 00:50:11,300
<i>millions of dollars were
illegally laundered</i>

753
00:50:11,383 --> 00:50:14,261
<i>from Chinese businessmen to
affect American elections.</i>

754
00:50:14,929 --> 00:50:16,055
<i>Is this true?</i>

755
00:50:17,139 --> 00:50:18,558
<i>Yes. That's true.</i>

756
00:50:20,309 --> 00:50:21,936
<i>And can you
tell us what, if any,</i>

757
00:50:22,019 --> 00:50:23,938
<i>was then Vice President
Francis Underwood's</i>

758
00:50:24,021 --> 00:50:25,522
<i>involvement in this scheme?</i>

759
00:50:26,982 --> 00:50:30,027
<i>Well, it appeared to me, he was
one of its chief architects.</i>

760
00:50:34,156 --> 00:50:37,159
And, uh, what exactly do
you mean by that, sir?

761
00:50:38,285 --> 00:50:39,411
It was his idea.

762
00:50:42,414 --> 00:50:44,667
Mark, I think you need to
be elsewhere right now.

763
00:50:44,750 --> 00:50:46,794
Are we gonna bury our heads
in the sand with this?

764
00:50:48,545 --> 00:50:50,089
No, you're going to leave,

765
00:50:50,172 --> 00:50:53,092
and the vice president and I are
going to decide what to do.

766
00:50:53,175 --> 00:50:54,802
Walker reneged. We had a deal.

767
00:50:54,885 --> 00:50:56,637
We will let you know if you are needed.

768
00:50:56,721 --> 00:50:58,764
- Sir...
- That was not a request.

769
00:51:03,894 --> 00:51:06,021
Why did you have to go talk to him?

770
00:51:08,608 --> 00:51:10,735
There has to be a way out, Francis.

771
00:51:13,237 --> 00:51:15,615
Does there? Is there?

772
00:51:16,991 --> 00:51:18,826
Or is this my presidency?

773
00:51:19,827 --> 00:51:21,954
Forever with a target on my back.

774
00:51:22,955 --> 00:51:24,624
Haunted and hunted.

775
00:51:37,803 --> 00:51:40,848
<i>LeAnn, if you're hearing
this, it means that I'm dead.</i>

